Third-party notices for lmshark
===============================

lmshark's own code (cpp/, schema/, scripts/, examples/, tests/; including the vendored `cpp/dslvm.hpp`
runtime from the cpp-dsl-sdk project and `cpp/memory_budget.{hpp,cpp}`) is Copyright (c) 2026 Lattice
Systems LLC, MIT license (see LICENSE). `lmshark --notices` prints this file.

There is no third-party packet-dissection or capture library in the binary: the pcap and pcapng readers
and writers, packet builder, Internet checksums, protocol dissectors (Ethernet, Linux SLL, BSD loopback, IEEE 802.1Q VLAN and 802.1ad QinQ, ARP,
IPv4, IPv6, ICMP, ICMPv6, TCP, UDP, DNS, HTTP/1.x, TLS ClientHello/ServerHello SNI & supported_versions,
DHCP, NTP, QUIC v1/v2), TCP stream reassembler, RTT and retransmission analyzer, conversation/flow tables,
and display/BPF filter evaluators are original MIT implementations written from the public IETF RFCs and
IEEE specifications.

No code from Wireshark, libwireshark, tshark, tcpdump, or GPL/LGPL libraries is included or linked.

Runtime libraries in static builds
----------------------------------
- Linux static builds link libstdc++/libgcc (GPL-3.0-with-GCC-exception, which permits static linking into
  any MIT executable) and glibc (LGPL-2.1-or-later, which permits static linking provided the user can
  relink against a modified glibc: the complete lmshark source and CMake build at
  https://github.com/lmctl-ai/lmshark are public, so the binary can be rebuilt and relinked with any glibc;
  glibc source at https://www.gnu.org/software/libc/).
- Windows static builds link the MinGW-w64 runtime and winpthreads (Zope Public License / MIT / BSD / public
  domain) and libstdc++/libgcc (GPL-3.0-with-GCC-exception).
